{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-163047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=2x{y}^{3}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-163047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b3a1db6fdfde8715aef4b8c8b0ff8d9dd30aef5b7de7ea9156c005383e0c5306"} {"question" "q-163047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-163047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2xy & 3xy\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3} & 2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}xy & {x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-163047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-163033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-163033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca