a256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-162944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}})={x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}} & {x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-162944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b3a1db6fdfde8715aef4b8c8b0ff8d9dd30aef5b7de7ea9156c005383e0c5306"} {"question" "q-162944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-162944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "q-162944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d61a415498a477cf836fbf5a14ce7eaf882f3864890ae27ce96fcca46040745"} {"question" "e-12477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/d8be5d5bd5fa2cd8b618087109678a4f891b08f3788d4946d07bc1b5e4b39a3a.png)\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/a2dbcf34ea50e451efc96ad2f9a7422a7ae507ca8f74088569110abb25293954.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12477.json" "file_sha256" "3800da2cc1e3f46333b13dcab5465abc9e90a3acafd18f71241e98ab5d202ab0"} {"question" "e-12477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/17588b058b1e1fe934e06679836234f0ef35fe4484a558fcc3ec3b2aafbd2620.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/613ba027e9564812a8e050efd69fd98fff3f614f7d2ec69eeb787c628ccabd12.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/87579b882ca6d8c92d4a7adfed575873fd07c36ab20251722c2cc8fc0f0328e8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/9fc2431aa874c68b23b6640da987b2f3bc37e2261936f7e1656b04bc3fc0030d.png)\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/b83c8f9cb4130103d63821f0d0de50ec0628138dcc8faaf16e8cf9fc27f1a994.png) is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/0851481c21c553b6e0e06a795815b1845e96e0a14f805e440529d0f4d6f85323.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12477.json" "file_sha256" "3800da2cc1e3f46333b13dcab5465abc9e90a3acafd18f71241e98ab5d202ab0"} {"question" "e-12478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/cf566a0b293a594fa46ae42ec2c4d348d0720b912ef6ef0e333dd3f05f204a9e.png)\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/5b729806def4b82ee72cd58bc41f39ceb2d1acad82d3a7098fec5bc6be925d6b.png)\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12478.json" "file_sha256" "69567168d6eba2a11908a9ec6defa6d64382a3136b8c1dd0e25563e2025ec699"} {"question" "e-12478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/0496b786dfda2338d951906d6fafb261e9aee95d9390423e69a5a55a76790918.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/bb37d8fdb7e4da90a22933c264d49ff999a3502618a6cd3725e8a5a2fd55a9ed.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/f2c5b815b56c791d1538c225c954c2fdc5603f879e4254838bb73994b6493d48.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/238be519e096397dafb2ad5b915d07a09f5129dc897b45955715e76da1146d1e.png)\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12478.json" "file_sha256" "69567168d6eba2a11908a9ec6defa6d64382a3136b8c1dd0e25563e2025ec699"} {"question" "e-12479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/f11de75b045b391687224fe118c2e1d3ec6d22e476ed030a3af10b55afb63eae.png)\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/11c6c7d42c15626ed4a04d31dabbb6a9dea649c25240c350c46eae80ea55f687.png) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/890997aca384f6846dda8a9465aa36a838f76d3db05eca1dbc7725cc03413599.png)\n\nfind the missing values." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12479.json" "file_sha256" "159cd5e9f9645907b818c60ad0fdd796d6eaa70ced9f1c58a17fe43cd42bfbb5"} {"question" "e-12479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/f18ed650b92e1d02e809d5eb997a231ec9a118da285292f88339bb4fdc3aaf95.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/613ba027e9564812a8e050efd69fd98fff3f614f7d2ec69eeb787c628ccabd12.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/87579b882ca6d8c92d4a7adfed575873fd07c36ab20251722c2cc8fc0f0328e8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})=3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/dcff4801886965e64a2e145869bd8c1ee4559c8cbe458661d53ad06ecd6ddb30.png)\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/e73720f8e1c08238a11ab557a3e515a214b3b9ed76000ca900ccf801de5d76c4.png) we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/6d7d1453ae6acad9a4d99359bcf40f4e90ac3383ade8a0aff58c82097fe37fde.png)\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/5c360a03ebca35811d9d95508cf4dc08481865040c64aa6f608202de88af98f2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12479.json" "file_sha256" "159cd5e9f9645907b818c60ad0fdd796d6eaa70ced9f1c58a17fe43cd42bfbb5"} {"question" "e-12484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/be3163cd89092fe54a0349a901b6bbd36829c265f1a1fc51493b8a992b8c13d1.png)\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/4043ac6ae5b5e2664350b31956c1c75f6d2441e5f2d48a284e3e5340e4824de4.png) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/04e15d28c62e16e51f6cf3eb5d7d2e84a5ac7f0bdf3b65e9d1e5f5baf71eb590.png)\n\nwhat are the missing values?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12484.json" "file_sha256" "4e75fd65f11867924fe12c2adfe28723a69d24b9d1f0266b386b954e09ab835a"} {"question" "e-12484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/e79145a13ada389ac337a69f8c48952f53b70d5caf48d950b305275ed8773b44.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/5366c8bcd23e435ac2ecb0dc523f8a828583486a46a246a92abf4762fc55e789.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/7bd197f9a502e48966fc902bba2d0fe8b8ddd3a0ce767d80b7a68b2a53a0aebb.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})={x}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/7b54f254580d3374c6535d0935f1ba8e4a7d5ca4eca0aab424900097dd217684.png)\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/22f4d34b8ec830740b285e1c31954b1e382264c4f366d3ea426ecadd3fd5f5bb.png) we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/651b14a10334bdfc073b86359ce306c1b4ba25b9390f345b609978e34cc08cad.png)\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/assets/3faf4b27b2df75a32661fb97309ee83c4d7bbd906729702119cba79bbf06b1f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068404/example-12484.json" "file_sha256" "4e75fd65f11867924fe12c2adfe28723a69d24b9d1f0266b386b954e09ab835a"}] "usage" {"e-12479" [] "e-12484" [] "q-162944" [] "e-12478" [] "q-162941" [] "e-12477" []} "basis" 2416 "retractions" [[17592186198559 :question/problem "For the transformation $f: R^{2} → R^{2}$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ e^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\ncalculate $f^{′}(x)$."] [17592186198562 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f: R^{2} → R^{2}$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1}x_{2} \\\\ e^{x_{1}x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$f(x) \\approx \\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2\\ln 2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix})$,\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [17592186197981 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186197981 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y) = 2xy, \\mid \\frac{∂f}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y) = x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & x^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(-1) & 1^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186197982 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ y^{2} \\\\ y\\ln x\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A A & x \\\\ A A & 2y \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & A A\\end{bmatrix}$\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?"] [17592186197982 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) = y, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) = x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2}) = 0, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2}) = 2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\ln x) = \\frac{y}{x}, & \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ 0 & 2y \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & ln ⁡ x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}$."] [17592186197983 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nfind the missing values."] [17592186197983 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} + e^{y}) = 3x^{2}, \\mid \\frac{∂f}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} + e^{y}) = e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} & e^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a)$, $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)$, and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & e 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186197984 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2}\\sin x_{1} \\\\ \\sin x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}π \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat are the missing values?"] [17592186197984 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{1}}(x_{2}\\sin x_{1}) = x_{2}\\cos x_{1}, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{2}}(x_{2}\\sin x_{1}) = \\sin x_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{1}}(\\sin x_{2}) = 0, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{2}}(\\sin x_{2}) = \\cos x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2}\\cos x_{1} & \\sin x_{1} \\\\ 0 & \\cos x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at $\\begin{bmatrix}π \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a)$, $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)$, and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194256 17592186194257 17592186194258 17592186194259 17592186194260 17592186194274 17592186194275 17592186194276 17592186194277 17592186194278] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194255 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194255 :answer-field/id] [17592186194255 :answer-field/key] [17592186194255 :answer-field/type] [17592186194273 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194273 :answer-field/id] [17592186194273 :answer-field/key] [17592186194273 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
